A||||8||solo2m4||setting up a solo car||||3||
Z||000000||solo2m4||12-13-2001||09:18 PM||||I was looking into setting up my 2m4 for parking lot racing, and wanted to know what makes the most diff for the buck. I have a good R rated tire on the way. Oh by the way the car will not be driven to the track<BR>I have tons of ideas but would like some input!!<BR>Thanx||||reg||3
Z||000001||Mach10||12-13-2001||10:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by solo2m4:<BR><B>I was looking into setting up my 2m4 for parking lot racing, and wanted to know what makes the most diff for the buck. I have a good R rated tire on the way. Oh by the way the car will not be driven to the track<BR>I have tons of ideas but would like some input!!<BR>Thanx</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What year is it?<P>-A few degrees camber on all 4 corners<BR>-sway bar on the back<BR>-remove steering dampner<BR>-go to a 4:10 tranny (if it doesn't already have it)<BR>-chop up the interior<BR>-remove spare, jack etc.<BR>-take out ANYTHing that you don't critically need.<P>All this should keep you in "stock" class, and cost you VERY little. don't bother with exhaust, intake is questionable. You need bottom-end grunt. the 4:10 4-spd will help you put whatever the motor has onto the pavement  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000002||solo2m4||12-13-2001||10:31 PM||||It is an 85 but it is a 5 speed<BR>I was thinking of putting a brake proportioning valve on, removing the factory gas tank and put a fuel cell in the front, moving the bat to the front, pulling off the alt, water pump, and all pulls to cut down on rotaional mass of the motor. puttting a small dc pump up front for cooling and I was going to charge the car in between runs. If I can find a place to park it inside I was going to ramove even the glass and doors and just pin the skins onto the car fiero door are a ton||||reg||1
Z||000003||cadero2dmax||12-13-2001||11:41 PM||||You can set a Fiero up in many ways, depending on what class you want to play in. Stock? your options are limited. Street Prepared, quite a bit more open. Prepared - wide open. Mod - - th sky is the limit.<BR>Then there is where I will play with the Cadero - Street Mod 2 will be a class in 2002.<P>Look on the SCCA site and read the rules (the 2002 rules weren't posted last I looked, but the levels of car prep won't change)and see where you fall - - then you will know what you can/cannot do to your car.<P>For a novice, i would suggest you do nothing to the car except a good alignment. Learn to drive the car fast first, then "fix it up".<P>How would you like to have a couple hundred other racers to ask questions of? An archives that will tell you EXACTLY what alignment specs to set your car to. What tire pressures a Fiero reacts best to, at least what pressures to start with. Racing photos, ad nauseum.<P>Join the FieroRacingList@yahoogroups.com<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000004||solo2m4||12-14-2001||12:20 AM||||I did go there I'm a pending member or something like that. If you know how I can get on, I would like the help. I have till spring to figer out what and how much I'm going to do,<BR>But the ideas that I have would be a good start to learn off yes no? and still keep me in stock!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||ITB 2M4||12-14-2001||12:20 AM||||I wouldn't remove the stock tank, it's sitting pretty low in the car as it is.  I'm running SCCA's road racing class, and I'm using the stock tank.  I've got a piece of sheet metal covering the tank to keep anything from puncturing it, and I havn't had any pick-up problems with the fuel pump.<P>I'm running a Koni suspension kit I bought from the Fiero Store for about a $1000.  It came with springs, sway bars, poly-urethane bushings, and Koni shocks and struts.  I also have a set of hard mounts for the rear cradle, and will be going to spherical bearings for the steering and rear locator rods soon.  Also, I'm running the Moog Problem Solver ball joints in the front.  You have to do a bit of work to the control arms to get the old pop riveted ball joints out, but it's well worth it.  I bought an aftermarket radiator, which is three core instead of two, and with the IRM front chin spoiler in place, it stays under 200 degrees at speed in the Florida summers.<P>One of the best things you can do is put an oil cooler on that 2.5L.  It will save you a lot of headaches down the road.  Anything you can do to increase the oil capacity of the 2.5L will help.  I'm running an Accusump 3qt. tank, along with a remote oil filter, and the oil cooler as well, for a grand total of 10qts.<P>If you decide to build a motor, you can use ARP rod bolts for a 283ci Chevy.  They are a tiny bit longer than the stock bolts, but it doesn't get into anything, and is a big improvement over the stock bolts.  Z-28 H.O. valve springs will work as well, along with the Oldsmobile spacers for valves.  The stock cyl. head has vanes in the intake runners, which block a little bit of the flow.  Get an older 2.5L head which doesn't have the vanes.  You will have to change the coolant filler neck from one end to the other, but it's a direct fit.  An MSD ignition system will help as well.  The fuel pump from a V-6 has better output, but it's a pain to pull the tank and replace it.  <P>I've totally gutted the interior on mine.  It's got an eight point roll cage, and it's almost too tight a fit for the aluminum seat.  I've got a plexi-glass window in the back, since the cage connects to the strut towers in the rear.  I have a 1/16th inch piece of aluminum across where the moonroof used to be.  Unfortunately, it's only making 88hp at the rear wheels right now, but I've got to take it to the chassis dyno again soon to 'tune' it.  It's making 110ft/lbs, so I may just have the timing off a bit.<P>Oh yeah, get the Holley aluminum timing gears.  The phenolic fiber gear doesn't like high rpm's at all, and has been known to jump around and totally disentigrate itself.  And if you're lucky enough to find them, get the Holley intake and TBI unit for the 2.5L.<P>Hope some of that helps, and it wasn't so long you lost interest.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||14
Z||000006||solo2m4||12-14-2001||12:50 PM||||Thax for the input I did not Know about the timming gear and the oil cooler if you know of a web page I  can go to to price it out I would also like To know if anyone has canadian sites or a places in toronto to go to !!||||reg||1
Z||000007||shark93726||12-14-2001||01:50 PM||||Mostly good advice by the previous posts, but get an SCCA Solo II rule book first, or read their online one, and make sure you are not making mods that will throw you into modified class if you intended to run stock or street prepared. (example, Stock and Street prepared do not allow ANY gutting of the interior)<P>The cheapest improvements that REALLY make an improvement, IMHO, are adding negative camber and extra positive caster, slight lowering, solid mounts for front Anti sway bar, and solid bushings throughout the suspension  and frame mounts. All of the other mods you were contemplating, would make hardly more improvement than ONE set of solid bushings on the front lower A-arms.<P>Next, in importance, by expense, would be getting D.O.T. racing tires.<P>Third, would be increasing horsepower and lightening the car.<P>The reason I list the mods in that order is because you can make the first group of mods and still run in Street prepared, while you save up for the more expensive mods, and, since it takes a little experience to learn how to drive a mid engine car well at the limit, you will have gained that experience by the time you start making the other mods, which will make you a better driver.<P>If you want specifics about alignment, suspension mods, etc. there is a fair amount of detail about it at my web site, http:/www.8shark.com/ and you can get much other info and help from these forums and others. (too many to list here)<P>I, and many others, believe the Fiero is potentially one of the best handling Autocross cars for the money, and with the right mods, can easily be a class winner in nearly any class you want to compete in. And it is defenitely one of the most fun to drive, because of it's excellent handling characteristics.<P>Have fun!!<P><P>------------------<BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||12-14-2001||09:20 PM||||Fun, yes.<P>Competitive, No.<P>The SCCA has the Fiero classed completely wrong, and has since 1993 when the Miata came on the scene. Even in the Prepared class - - well, just let it suffice to say that my Q4 had over $25,000 in it all total, every trick and mod I could think of and do -- and it still was not Nationally competitive. Four time Midwest Division champion, both my wife and I were in Prepared. But Nationally - - -<P>And that ain't all the driver, either. My son is an 8 time trophy winner at Nationals - - he can drive. But the one time he drove for me he couldn't do well in A Prepared against the racing Porsches, 427 Cobra's, Sunbeam Tigers, and TVR's. Nor could I.<P>Just enjoy your car, it is the most fun machine on the autocross course I have ever driven (except maybe a Formula Atlantic) or on a track (except my favorite track car, a Legend). <P>If you want something Nationally competitive, better look elsewhere. A Fiero hasn't won a trophy since 1992 at the Nationals -- the Tamandli brothers excepted in their Formula chassis and Rover racing engine (a 1500 pound car with GT body panels). They are 5 time National Champs in E-Mod.<P>G <P><p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
